Good news: the JSON Patch you already compute gives you field-level paths (`/employment/0/title`), so "which section changed" is mostly a matter of **defining sections** and **grouping the diff by them**. But if you do it right you also get a big performance win: per-section hashes let you decide *which* sections changed without diffing at all, and let clients subscribe to only the sections they care about.

## 1. Define the section schema

Make sections explicit config, not implicit "top-level key = section" (top-level keys drift; sections are a product concept).

```yaml
snapshot:
  sections:
    identity:   ["/firstName", "/lastName", "/dateOfBirth", "/gender"]
    contact:    ["/emails", "/phones"]
    address:    ["/addresses"]
    employment: ["/employment"]
    education:  ["/education"]
    social:     ["/profiles", "/website"]
  default-section: other      # anything unmapped lands here
```

```java
@ConfigurationProperties("snapshot")
public record SectionSchema(Map<String, List<String>> sections, String defaultSection) {

    /** Longest-prefix match: "/employment/3/title" -> "employment" */
    public String sectionOf(String pointer) {
        String best = defaultSection; int bestLen = -1;
        for (var e : sections.entrySet())
            for (String p : e.getValue())
                if ((pointer.equals(p) || pointer.startsWith(p + "/")) && p.length() > bestLen) {
                    best = e.getKey(); bestLen = p.length();
                }
        return best;
    }

    /** Extract just one section's subtree as a small object (used for hashing and diffing). */
    public ObjectNode extract(JsonNode state, String section, ObjectMapper om) {
        ObjectNode out = om.createObjectNode();
        for (String p : sections.get(section)) {
            JsonNode n = state.at(p);
            if (!n.isMissingNode()) out.set(p, n);        // key = pointer, keeps paths stable
        }
        return out;
    }
}
```

## 2. Normalize arrays of entities → keyed maps (important for meaningful diffs)

Index-based patches (`/emails/2/value`) are useless to a human and unstable if an item is removed. Before hashing/diffing, convert arrays whose elements have an id into objects keyed by that id:

```java
public final class Canonicalizer {
    private static final List<String> ID_FIELDS = List.of("id", "uuid", "key");

    public static JsonNode canonicalize(JsonNode n, ObjectMapper om) {
        if (n.isObject()) {
            ObjectNode o = om.createObjectNode();
            // sorted keys → deterministic hashing
            StreamSupport.stream(Spliterators.spliteratorUnknownSize(n.fieldNames(), 0), false)
                .sorted().forEach(f -> o.set(f, canonicalize(n.get(f), om)));
            return o;
        }
        if (n.isArray() && n.size() > 0 && n.get(0).isObject() && idField(n.get(0)) != null) {
            String idf = idField(n.get(0));
            ObjectNode keyed = om.createObjectNode();
            n.forEach(el -> keyed.set(el.get(idf).asText(), canonicalize(el, om)));
            return keyed;                                   // /emails/{id}/value
        }
        if (n.isArray()) {
            ArrayNode a = om.createArrayNode();
            n.forEach(el -> a.add(canonicalize(el, om)));
            return a;
        }
        return n;
    }
    private static String idField(JsonNode o) {
        return ID_FIELDS.stream().filter(o::has).findFirst().orElse(null);
    }
}
```

Apply this in `normalize()` (together with stripping volatile fields) — both the stored snapshot and incoming events go through it, so they're always comparable.

## 3. Per-section hashes

```java
@Component @RequiredArgsConstructor
public class SectionHasher {
    private final SectionSchema schema; private final ObjectMapper om;

    public Map<String, String> hash(JsonNode canonicalState) {
        Map<String, String> out = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (String s : schema.sections().keySet())
            out.put(s, sha256(schema.extract(canonicalState, s, om).toString()));
        return out;
    }
    public static Set<String> changed(Map<String,String> before, Map<String,String> after) {
        return after.entrySet().stream()
            .filter(e -> !e.getValue().equals(before.get(e.getKey())))
            .map(Map.Entry::getKey).collect(Collectors.toCollection(LinkedHashSet::new));
    }
}
```

Store these in three places:

```java
// SnapshotDoc – add:
@Field(type = FieldType.Object) private Map<String, String> sectionHashes;   // dynamic keyword fields
@Field(type = FieldType.Keyword) private Set<String> watchSections;          // empty = all
```

```java
// PersonStateStore – Redis hash "pstate:{personId}" with one field per section + "v"
public Mono<Map<String,String>> sectionHashes(String personId) {
    return redis.opsForHash().entries("pstate:" + personId)
        .collectMap(e -> (String) e.getKey(), e -> (String) e.getValue());
}
public Mono<Void> put(String personId, Map<String,String> hashes, long v) {
    Map<String,String> m = new HashMap<>(hashes); m.put("v", Long.toString(v));
    return redis.opsForHash().putAll("pstate:" + personId, m).then();
}
```

