## 5. Updated processor

```java
public Mono<Void> process(String personId, byte[] payload, long version) {
    JsonNode current = normalize(om.readTree(payload));          // strip volatile + canonicalize
    Map<String,String> curHashes = hasher.hash(current);

    return personState.sectionHashes(personId)
        .defaultIfEmpty(Map.of())
        .flatMap(prev -> {
            Set<String> globallyChanged = SectionHasher.changed(prev, curHashes);
            if (globallyChanged.isEmpty()) {                     // fast path: nothing changed anywhere
                metrics.counter("events.noop").increment();
                return Mono.empty();
            }
            globallyChanged.forEach(s -> metrics.counter("sections.changed", "section", s).increment());
            return fanOut(personId, current, curHashes, version)
                .then(personState.put(personId, curHashes, version));
        });
}

private Mono<Void> fanOut(String personId, JsonNode current, Map<String,String> curHashes, long version) {
    return subscribers.clientsFor(personId).collectList()
        .filter(l -> !l.isEmpty())
        .flatMapMany(ids -> snapshots.mget(ids.stream().map(c -> c + ":" + personId).toList()))
        .filter(doc -> version > doc.getLastNotifiedVersion())
        .flatMap(doc -> {
            // which sections changed *for this client* (vs. what they were last told)
            Set<String> changed = SectionHasher.changed(doc.getSectionHashes(), curHashes);
            if (doc.getWatchSections() != null && !doc.getWatchSections().isEmpty())
                changed.retainAll(doc.getWatchSections());       // client-level section filter
            if (changed.isEmpty()) return Mono.empty();          // e.g. only "social" changed, client doesn't care
            return notifyAndAdvance(doc, current, curHashes, changed, version);
        }, 32)
        .then();
}

private Mono<Void> notifyAndAdvance(SnapshotDoc doc, JsonNode current, Map<String,String> curHashes,
                                    Set<String> changedSections, long version) {
    JsonNode baseline = om.valueToTree(doc.getLastNotifiedState() != null
                                       ? doc.getLastNotifiedState() : doc.getSnapshot());

    // Diff ONLY the changed sections – small inputs, no work for unchanged ones
    List<SectionChange> changes = changedSections.stream()
        .map(s -> new SectionChange(s, diffSection(baseline, current, s)))
        .filter(sc -> !sc.fields().isEmpty())
        .toList();
    if (changes.isEmpty()) return Mono.empty();

    var n = new ChangeNotification(doc.getClientId(), doc.getPersonId(), version,
        doc.getClientId() + ":" + doc.getPersonId() + ":" + version, doc.getSnapshotAt(), changes);

    return notifier.send(n)
        .then(Mono.defer(() -> {
            doc.setLastNotifiedState(om.convertValue(current, Map.class));
            doc.setSectionHashes(curHashes);
            doc.setLastNotifiedHash(overallHash(curHashes));
            doc.setLastNotifiedVersion(version);
            return snapshots.save(doc);
        }))
        .onErrorResume(OptimisticLockingFailureException.class, e -> Mono.empty())
        .then();
}

private static final EnumSet<DiffFlags> FLAGS = EnumSet.of(
    DiffFlags.OMIT_MOVE_OPERATION, DiffFlags.OMIT_COPY_OPERATION,
    DiffFlags.ADD_ORIGINAL_VALUE_ON_REPLACE, DiffFlags.ADD_EXPLICIT_REMOVE_ADD_ON_REPLACE);

private List<FieldChange> diffSection(JsonNode base, JsonNode cur, String section) {
    // Diff the full docs but keep only ops that fall in this section → correct paths, cheap enough;
    // or diff schema.extract(...) of each side if sections are large.
    JsonNode patch = JsonDiff.asJson(base, cur, FLAGS);
    List<FieldChange> out = new ArrayList<>();
    for (JsonNode op : patch) {
        String path = op.get("path").asText();
        if (!schema.sectionOf(path).equals(section)) continue;
        out.add(new FieldChange(path, op.get("op").asText(),
            op.path("fromValue").isMissingNode() ? null : op.get("fromValue"),
            op.path("value").isMissingNode()     ? null : op.get("value")));
    }
    return out;
}
```

(Cache the full-doc patch once per `(doc, current)` pair rather than recomputing it per section — trivially done with a local variable; I split it above for clarity.)

## 6. Bookmark API gains section subscriptions

```java
public record BookmarkRequest(Set<String> watchSections) {}   // null/empty = everything

@PostMapping("/{personId}")
public Mono<SnapshotDoc> bookmark(@PathVariable String clientId, @PathVariable String personId,
                                  @RequestBody(required = false) BookmarkRequest req) {
    return people.current(personId).map(state -> {
        JsonNode canon = normalize(om.valueToTree(state));
        SnapshotDoc d = new SnapshotDoc();
        ...
        d.setSectionHashes(hasher.hash(canon));
        d.setWatchSections(req == null ? Set.of() : validate(req.watchSections()));
        return d;
    }).flatMap(snapshots::save).flatMap(d -> subscribers.add(clientId, personId).thenReturn(d));
}

@PatchMapping("/{personId}/watch")   // change what they watch later
public Mono<Void> updateWatch(...) { ... }
```

And a read endpoint clients will want: **"what's different between my snapshot and now?"** — the same `diffSection` over `snapshot` (the frozen one) vs. current state, grouped by section. That's the UI's "3 sections changed since you saved" badge.

## 7. Hot-person optimization (optional, ES does the filtering)

For a person with 100k bookmarks, most clients don't watch the section that changed, or were already notified. Let Elasticsearch pre-filter instead of `mget`-ing everything:

```json
{
  "query": { "bool": {
    "filter": [ { "term": { "personId": "p-123" } } ],
    "should": [
      { "bool": { "must_not": { "term": { "sectionHashes.employment": "<h_emp>" } },
                  "filter":   { "bool": { "should": [
                      { "term": { "watchSections": "employment" } },
                      { "bool": { "must_not": { "exists": { "field": "watchSections" } } } }
                  ]}}}},
      { "bool": { "must_not": { "term": { "sectionHashes.contact": "<h_contact>" } },
                  "filter":   { ... same pattern for "contact" ... } } }
    ],
    "minimum_should_match": 1
  }},
  "_source": ["clientId", "sectionHashes", "watchSections", "lastNotifiedState", "lastNotifiedVersion"]
}
```

Build one `should` clause per *globally changed* section (you already know that set from the Redis fast path). Only the docs that actually need a notification come back. Paginate with `search_after` on `_id` for large result sets. Since the ES refresh interval is a few seconds, a doc you just advanced may show up once more — the in-memory `changed` check in `fanOut` catches it, so this is safe.

## 8. Things to decide

- **Section granularity for arrays**: is adding a new job "employment changed" or do you want sub-sections like `employment.current` vs `employment.history`? The schema handles it — just add pointers with longer prefixes; longest match wins.
- **Severity per section**: put `priority: high|normal` in the schema and let the notifier route (push vs. digest). Easy add to `SectionChange`.
- **Renamed/moved records**: with id-keyed arrays, an entity whose id changes shows as remove + add. If your upstream doesn't have stable ids, pick a composite key (e.g. `company+startDate`) in the canonicalizer.
- **Digesting**: if one person emits 10 events in a minute, clients get 10 notifications. If that's noisy, buffer per `(clientId, personId)` with `bufferTimeout` / a short Redis debounce and merge the section sets — the rolling baseline design makes merging trivial since you just diff baseline → latest.

